By the end of this module, the learner should be able to:

Explain purpose, vision, mission, goals and objectives;

Distinguish purpose from a job, business or position;

Identify personal sources of meaning and contribution;

Formulate a practical personal purpose statement;

create a meaningful personal vision;

Translate vision into strategic goals;

Distinguish short-, medium- and long-term goals;

Develop measurable objectives;

Create a personal development plan;

Prioritize competing demands;

Identify the resources and capabilities required for their direction;

Create an actionable 90-day plan;

Create a broader 1-, 3- and 5-year direction;

Review and adjust goals when circumstances change;

produce a Personal Purpose, Vision & Growth Blueprint.

Imagine two people walking ten kilometres.

The first person walks quickly but does not know where they are going.

The second person walks at a moderate pace toward a clearly identified destination.

Who is more likely to arrive where they intended?

The lesson is simple:

Speed without direction can increase the distance between you and your desired destination.

This applies to:

Education;

Careers;

Business;

Finance;

Relationships;

Leadership;

Personal development;

Organizations;

Government institutions.

Being busy is not the same as making progress.

Progress requires movement toward something meaningful.

Do not trap yourself by believing:

"I must discover one job that represents my entire purpose."

Human beings can contribute in multiple ways.

A person may:

Build a career;

Raise a family;

Serve a community;

Build a business;

Teach;

Create products;

Lead an organization;

Support government;

Participate in humanitarian work.

Your purpose can provide continuity while your roles change.

Therefore:

Your role may change without destroying your direction.

Purpose should not be treated only as personal fulfillment.

Mature purpose includes responsibility.

Ask:

Who benefits from:

The work you want to do;

The life you want to build;

The people you want to serve;

The contribution you want to make;

The assets or institutions you want to build;

The impact you want to create.

Vision gives direction to ambition.

A strong personal vision should answer:

Who am I becoming?

What kind of life am I building?

What capabilities will I possess?

What kind of work will I be doing?

What value will I create?

Who will benefit?

What will success look like?

What will I have built?

What will I be known for?

Do not make your vision only about possessions.

A vision such as:

"I want to own a big house and a luxury car."

describes possessions, not a comprehensive life direction.

A stronger vision may include:

character;

relationships;

health;

learning;

career;

business;

financial independence;

service;

leadership;

contribution;

impact.

A goal is a meaningful result you intend to achieve.

Examples:

Obtain a professional qualification;

Increase income;

Launch a business;

Improve customer acquisition;

Secure employment;

Develop a technical skill;

Build a professional network;

Improve an organizational process;

Complete a major project.

Goals create focus, without goals, intentions often remain vague.

A meaningful goal should be:

Relevant;

Important;

Sufficiently challenging;

Understandable;

Measurable where appropriate;

Time-bound;

Connected to a larger direction.

A useful framework is SMART:

Specific -What exactly am I trying to achieve?

Measurable - How will I know whether I achieved it?

Achievable - Is it realistically achievable given the resources and constraints?

Relevant - Does it contribute to an important outcome?

Time-bound - When should it be achieved?

SMART is a useful starting framework, but do not allow the framework to replace strategic thinking.

A perfectly written irrelevant goal is still a bad goal.

Different time horizons require different levels of detail.

SHORT TERM

Usually the next few weeks or months.

Focus:

Immediate priorities;

Habits;

Projects;

Skill development;

Urgent opportunities.

MEDIUM TERM

Usually approximately one to three years.

Focus:

Career development;

Business growth;

Financial milestones;

Qualifications;

Leadership development;

Major projects.

LONG TERM

Often three to five years or beyond.

Focus:

Major life direction;

Institutional ambitions;

Professional positioning;

Wealth-building;

Major business expansion;

Long-term contribution.

Long-term plans should provide direction without pretending that you can predict everything.

A Personal Development Plan, or PDP, is a structured system for developing the capabilities required for your goals.

Your PDP should identify:

Current capability;

Desired capability;

Development gap;

Learning required;

Practice required;

Resources;

Support;

Deadline;

Measurement;

Evidence.

Use:

Current State → Desired State → Gap → Development Action → Evidence

For every major goal ask:

"What must I become capable of doing to achieve this?"

For example, if the goal is to start a business, capabilities may include:

Customer research;

Product/service design;

Sales;

Marketing;

Financial management;

Negotiation;

Operations;

Digital tools.

If the goal is career advancement:

Technical expertise;

Communication;

Leadership;

Networking;

Project management;

Professional credentials;

Strategic thinking.

Goals should therefore create a development agenda.

Every "yes" consumes resources that cannot be used elsewhere.

If you say yes to:

Another project;

Another meeting;

Another course;

Another business opportunity;

You are also saying no, at least temporarily, to something else.

Mature planning recognizes trade-offs.

The question is not:

"Can I do everything?"

The question is:

"What deserves my limited time, attention, money and energy most?"

At the end of every month:

Review goals;

Review measurable results;

Review finances where relevant;

Review habits;

Review skills;

Review relationships;

Review opportunities;

Review obstacles;

Review priorities;

Adjust the next month's plan.

Every 90 days ask:

"Am I becoming the person and building the future I intended?"

Review:

Purpose;

Vision;

Goals;

Capability;

Results;

Finances;

Career/business;

Relationships;

Health;

Contribution;

Major risks.

Then decide:

Continue → Improve → Stop → Start

